// CCAR-P Revision — Domain 4: Evaluation, Testing & Optimization (16%) — 40 items
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id:"d4-01", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A team must evaluate two things: (1) whether outputs comply with a required JSON schema, and (2) whether reply tone is appropriately empathetic. Which evaluation approach fits?",
  options:["Model-based evals for both, with the schema included in the judge's rubric","Code-based evals for both","Code-based checks for schema compliance; model-based (LLM-as-judge) for tone","Code-based for schema; exact-match comparison against reference replies for tone"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Deterministic properties belong to cheap, reliable code-based checks; subjective qualities need model-based judgment. A unified judge rubric (A) wastes money on what code checks perfectly; code cannot score empathy (B); exact-match (D) cannot capture the many valid empathetic phrasings." },
{ id:"d4-02", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"After an A/B test on 40 conversations, a new prompt scores 82% satisfaction vs 78% for the old one. The PM wants to ship immediately. What should the architect recommend?",
  options:["Ship — the new prompt is proven better","Check whether the sample size makes the difference statistically meaningful before deciding","Segment the 40 conversations by topic to identify exactly where the new prompt performs best","Ship to 100% but keep the old prompt in version control for rollback"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A 4-point gap on n=40 is well within noise; required sample size should be estimated before reading the result as a win. Shipping (A, D) overclaims on an underpowered test; post-hoc segmentation of a tiny sample (C) makes the statistics worse." },
{ id:"d4-03", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"exam-guide-sample",
  question:"A RAG system suddenly returns confident but incorrect answers after a document refresh, while latency and model version are unchanged. What is the most likely first place to investigate?",
  options:["The model weights have silently changed","The retrieval/indexing step is returning irrelevant or stale chunks","The temperature setting is too low","The context window has shrunk"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Official guide rationale: confident-but-wrong answers following a document refresh, with model and latency unchanged, point to retrieval feeding the model poor context — for example a broken re-index or mismatched embeddings. The other options would not be triggered specifically by a document refresh." },
{ id:"d4-04", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A production assistant has explicit targets for answer quality, p95 response time, per-request cost, harmful-output rate, and prompt-injection resistance. The team measures only answer accuracy. What is incomplete?",
  options:["Add thresholds for latency, cost, harmful outputs, and injection resistance","Replace accuracy with satisfaction and treat operations as diagnostics","Keep accuracy as the gate and report operations after launch","Use the accuracy threshold as a proxy for the remaining requirements"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"The stated requirements make this a multidimensional evaluation: task quality, service performance, spend, safety, and security each need a measurable threshold. Satisfaction alone does not replace them, and a higher accuracy target cannot reveal independent operational or security regressions." },
{ id:"d4-05", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Average latency is 1.2 s, comfortably inside the 2 s SLA, yet users regularly complain about slow responses. What is the team most likely measuring wrong?",
  options:["Report median latency because it is less sensitive to a few slow requests","Measure server processing time separately and use it as the user-facing SLA, excluding network segments the team does not control","Group latency by response length but continue reporting the overall mean","Track tail latency (p95/p99), which reveals slow requests hidden by the mean"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Averages and medians can hide the slow tail that drives complaints; p95/p99 exposes it. Server-only timing omits part of user-perceived latency, and grouping by response length does not replace a percentile-based service measure." },
{ id:"d4-06", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"What is the recommended composition of a golden evaluation dataset?",
  options:["A random production sample with no deliberate coverage of rare failures","Synthetic edge cases with no production examples or independently validated labels, so no confidential data enters the set","Representative real cases plus edge and adversarial inputs, with expected outcomes","Recent failure reports with successful routine cases removed from the set"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"A golden set earns trust by covering the real distribution and its hard edges with defined expectations. An unstratified random sample can miss rare failures, synthetic-only coverage can drift from production reality, and a failure-only set distorts the target distribution." },
{ id:"d4-07", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Six months after launch, the eval set no longer resembles what users actually ask. What is the standard maintenance practice?",
  options:["Refresh on a fixed annual schedule using the original sampling frame","Sample production traffic, label a slice, and fold it into the eval set","Keep the original set unchanged and track production drift in a separate dashboard","Replace older cases with recent failures until the set matches current error patterns"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Eval sets are living assets fed by production sampling and labeling. A fixed annual refresh can lag current use, a separate drift dashboard does not repair stale test coverage, and replacing the set with failures alone makes it unrepresentative." },
{ id:"d4-08", domain:4, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A team used the candidate system itself to generate 5,000 eval cases and expected answers. Which TWO actions best reduce self-confirming test bias?",
  options:["Tune the candidate on all generated cases before scoring it","Reserve independently authored cases as a final held-out set","Accept generated expected answers whenever the candidate matches them","Expose the final holdout during prompt debugging so failures can be fixed","Have independent reviewers audit a stratified sample of cases and labels"],
  correct:[1,4],
  explanation:"An independently authored holdout limits contamination from the candidate's own behavior, while an independent audit can catch self-confirming cases or labels. Tuning on generated cases, accepting matching labels without review, or exposing the final holdout compromises the evidence." },
{ id:"d4-09", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Before trusting an LLM-as-judge for tone evaluation, what must the team do?",
  options:["Rely on the judge vendor's benchmark results as sufficient evidence","Calibrate it against human ratings on a sample until agreement is acceptable","Run the judge repeatedly and accept it if scores are internally consistent, since stable scores demonstrate reliability","Compare its scores with ratings produced by the candidate model itself"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A judge is an instrument: it must be calibrated against relevant human ground truth before its scores steer decisions. Vendor benchmarks may not match the task, repeated scoring tests consistency rather than validity, and candidate-model ratings are not an independent reference." },
{ id:"d4-10", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A judge model consistently scores outputs generated by its own model family higher than equivalent outputs from others. What is this, and what mitigates it?",
  options:["Rubric drift — rewrite the criteria and recalibrate both judge families from scratch","Sampling variance — repeat judgments and average every score","Position bias — randomize candidate order in each comparison","Self-preference bias — use an independent judge and human checks"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"A systematic preference for the judge's own model family is self-preference bias. An independent judge, explicit rubric, and human comparisons reduce that risk; repeated scoring or order randomization addresses different sources of error." },
{ id:"d4-11", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Two reviewers score the same outputs 'good' and 'poor' respectively, and eval results swing week to week. What is the foundational fix?",
  options:["An explicit rubric: defined criteria and what each score level requires","Average the two reviewer scores without changing the evaluation guidance","Give the more experienced reviewer greater weight without aligning criteria","Increase the reviewed sample size while retaining the current labels and guidance"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Rating disagreement of this kind signals missing shared criteria; an explicit rubric makes scores interpretable and comparable. Averaging, reviewer weighting, or a larger sample can preserve or amplify inconsistent judgments when the scoring standard itself remains undefined." },
{ id:"d4-12", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"How should the eval suite relate to the deployment pipeline of a production Claude system?",
  options:["Run the full suite before major releases and rely on production monitoring for smaller changes","Run evals after deployment and roll back changes that fail them","Run evals automatically on every applicable change as a regression gate before deployment","Run model-quality evals outside CI so delivery continues while results are reviewed"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Evals should be continuous, automated, and blocking for applicable changes—the AI equivalent of a CI regression suite. Major-release-only, post-deployment, or nonblocking evaluation allows known test failures to reach production before action is taken." },
{ id:"d4-13", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A significant prompt change passed offline evals. Which rollout plan best controls production risk while moving toward full traffic?",
  options:["Use a full rollout with rollback triggered only by user complaints","Keep a permanent internal-only deployment without customer validation, eliminating all external release risk","Hold a fixed 10% rollout without stop or expansion criteria","Use a monitored canary with stop conditions and evidence-based expansion"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"A canary limits exposure while predefined guardrails determine whether to stop, roll back, or expand. A full rollout has a larger blast radius, an indefinite internal pilot never validates customer traffic, and a fixed slice without decision criteria is not a controlled progression." },
{ id:"d4-14", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"An A/B test targets response quality as its primary metric. What else must be monitored during the test?",
  options:["Predefined guardrails for latency, cost, safety, and error rate","Predefined secondary quality metrics without operational or safety limits","Aggregate latency and error metrics without separating results by assigned variant","Guardrails chosen during analysis after the primary result is known"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"The primary metric tests the hypothesis; predefined guardrails prevent a local quality gain from hiding unacceptable regressions elsewhere. Quality-only secondary metrics leave operational risk uncovered, aggregate metrics hide variant effects, and post-result selection invites biased interpretation." },
{ id:"d4-15", domain:4, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Which TWO practices support valid inference in a live A/B test?",
  options:["Randomly assign eligible traffic between the variants","Stop the first time the preferred variant takes the lead","Change the primary metric when a secondary metric looks stronger","Predeclare the hypothesis, primary metric, guardrails, and stopping or sample plan","Send the new variant only to the most engaged users"],
  correct:[0,3],
  explanation:"Random assignment controls selection bias, while a predeclared analysis and stopping plan controls result-driven decisions. Optional stopping, metric switching, and assignment by engagement can all create an apparent effect that the treatment did not cause." },
{ id:"d4-16", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A properly powered A/B test finds no significant difference between the current prompt and a more elaborate new one. What is the correct decision?",
  options:["Adopt the new prompt because added detail is an improvement","Repeat the same test until a significant result appears","Keep the simpler variant because no measured benefit justifies its added cost","Split traffic permanently so the team need not make a final choice"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"The test provides no evidence that the extra complexity improves the target metric, so the lower-cost incumbent remains preferable. Repetition until significance is invalid, and a permanent split preserves cost and operational complexity without a demonstrated benefit." },
{ id:"d4-17", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"An assistant's answers cite document identifiers that do not exist anywhere in the corpus, while the retrieval logs show healthy, relevant chunks were provided. What is the diagnosis?",
  options:["A retrieval-side failure — the index needs a full rebuild from scratch","A context-window overflow","A latency problem upstream","Hallucination at generation, despite good retrieved context"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Healthy retrieval plus fabricated references localizes the fault to generation, not search. A blames the component the logs just cleared, and B and C do not produce invented identifiers." },
{ id:"d4-18", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A rule added last week works in a controlled playground test with the intended model and settings but is ignored in production. What should be checked first?",
  options:["Whether production actually runs the prompt version containing the rule","Whether the model version changed at the same time","Whether the rule conflicts with an earlier instruction somewhere in the prompt's history","Whether the traffic mix shifted toward harder queries"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"First verify that production loaded the intended prompt version. If it did, compare the production model ID, settings, complete instruction stack, and context with the controlled test; playground success does not by itself eliminate every environment difference." },
{ id:"d4-19", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Across multiple prompt rewrites, a mid-tier model handles self-contained simple cases but consistently fails self-contained multi-hop cases that include all required evidence. What does this pattern indicate?",
  options:["Prompt underspecification because the instructions do not prescribe a reasoning format","Model mismatch: the task's reasoning demands exceed the tier","Sampling instability caused by the decoding settings on longer answers","Retrieval mismatch because the hard cases require evidence absent from context"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Failure that tracks reasoning complexity, survives prompt iteration, and occurs with all evidence present points to model capability. A mandated reasoning format does not create missing capability, decoding instability would not explain a consistent complexity boundary, and the stem rules out absent retrieval evidence." },
{ id:"d4-20", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Cost per conversation is rising. Price, model, request mix, average turns, output length, retries, and tool-call rates are unchanged. Logs show uncached input tokens increasing on later turns and zero cache reads. What is the primary cause?",
  options:["Growing conversation history is resent without effective caching","Generated answers contain more output tokens than the baseline, which compounds across every conversation turn","Retry and tool loops execute more often than the baseline","The provider charges a higher price for the same token volume"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"The telemetry attributes the increase to uncached input growth from retained history. The stem controls output length, retries, tools, pricing, and workload mix, so those alternatives do not explain the observed per-turn token growth." },
{ id:"d4-21", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"For a chat product with a long repeated prefix, which pair best reduces repeated-prefix processing and improves perceived responsiveness?",
  options:["A faster model plus a non-streaming full-response buffer","Prompt caching for the stable prefix plus response streaming","A larger output limit plus additional retry attempts","Message batching plus longer retained conversation history, so each request carries maximal grounding"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Caching can reduce processing time and TTFT when the prefix matches and meets cache requirements. Streaming does not shorten server-side TTFT; it improves perceived latency by displaying generated output before the full response is complete." },
{ id:"d4-22", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A high-volume interactive workload repeats an identical 12,000-token prefix within five minutes, records no cache reads, and already meets its quality target. Which change most directly addresses the observed waste with the least quality risk?",
  options:["Trim retrieved evidence after a relevance-ablation study","Route a simple request class to a smaller model after non-inferiority testing","Cache the stable prefix and monitor cache reads, latency, and cost","Batch noninteractive workloads while leaving this interactive path unchanged"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"This workload supplies the conditions that make caching the direct experiment: a long identical prefix, reuse within the cache lifetime, and no current reads. Cache writes and misses still have costs, so the team must verify cache-read tokens, hit rate, latency, and spend rather than assume savings." },
{ id:"d4-23", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Before starting an optimization effort, what must the team establish?",
  options:["A target model to migrate to","An agreed percentage target for the reduction, set upfront","A shortlist of prompts to rewrite first","A measured baseline of tokens, latency, cost, and quality"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Optimization without a baseline cannot demonstrate improvement or catch regressions. A presumes the conclusion, a target (B) is meaningless without a starting point, and a rewrite list (C) picks solutions before measurement says where the spend actually is." },
{ id:"d4-24", domain:4, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A high-volume interactive service has no reusable prompt prefix. Evaluation shows that a well-defined simple request class passes on a smaller model, and many acceptable responses exceed the product's required length. Which TWO changes should the team test to reduce spend without weakening quality?",
  options:["Route the validated simple class to the smaller model and monitor its non-inferiority guardrails","Batch all interactive traffic into delayed jobs and compare only total daily throughput","Define a concise output contract with a tested token limit, then rerun quality and truncation evals","Remove a fixed percentage of retrieved evidence and use lower token count as the acceptance criterion","Disable response validation on the simple class, since the smaller model already passed its evals"],
  correct:[0,2],
  explanation:"Eval-gated routing right-sizes the model for a class already shown to preserve quality. A tested output contract can also reduce unnecessary generation, provided truncation and task-quality evals remain within limits. Delayed batching violates interactivity, and token reduction alone does not prove that uniformly trimmed evidence preserves correctness." },
{ id:"d4-25", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"One release changed the prompt, model ID, retrieval index, and tool schemas. A release manifest identifies the exact bundle, but a bad output appears after deployment. Which response best limits harm and attributes the regression?",
  options:["Keep the full release live while collecting enough failures for correlation analysis","Restore the known-good bundle, then replay the trace while varying one component at a time","Roll back the prompt and model together, then attribute any recovery to the prompt","Replace the model and index together, then compare quality by deployment"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Restoring the known-good bundle limits exposure; controlled trace replay and one-component-at-a-time comparisons then support causal attribution. Leaving the release live increases harm, while changing or rolling back selected components together cannot isolate the cause." },
{ id:"d4-26", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Which situation most warrants an automated alert rather than discovery at the next weekly review?",
  options:["A refusal-rate jump from 2% to 15% within an hour of a deployment","A 0.3% week-over-week drift in average response length","One user giving a thumbs-down","A quarter-long gradual rise in the average length of conversations"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"A sharp post-deployment refusal spike signals a live regression harming users now — the definition of alert-worthy. Slow drift (B, D) belongs in periodic review, and a single rating (C) is below any actionable threshold." },
{ id:"d4-27", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Quality metrics decline gradually over months with no deployments on the team's side. Which monitoring practice detects the likely cause?",
  options:["Model-serving resource monitoring for capacity-related output degradation","Weekly aggregate quality tracking without input-feature comparisons","Frequent reruns of the unchanged offline eval set against the same distribution","Input-distribution monitoring against the eval set to detect drift"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"With the application unchanged, gradual degradation can reflect input drift away from the eval distribution. Resource monitoring tests a serving hypothesis, aggregate quality confirms the symptom, and rerunning the same stale distribution cannot reveal how production inputs changed." },
{ id:"d4-28", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A candidate configuration raises answer accuracy by 3 points but breaches defined p95 latency and cost limits and doubles the prompt-injection bypass rate. What should the architect conclude?",
  options:["Ship because the primary quality metric improved materially","Average all metrics into one score and ship if the mean improves","Hold the rollout because required operational and security guardrails failed","Raise the breached thresholds to the observed values and approve the change, so alerts reflect the system's real behavior"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Success criteria are use-case-specific, but defined guardrails are release constraints rather than optional context. The quality gain does not compensate for breached latency, cost, and security limits; the team should iterate and retest instead of averaging away or redefining failures." },
{ id:"d4-29", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A team builds its evaluation set only from responses that received a thumbs-down. What is the main measurement risk and remedy?",
  options:["Selection bias; add a stratified sample across tasks and feedback states","Reviewer variance; ask each dissatisfied user to rate the same output twice","Cache drift; group every rating by whether the prompt prefix produced a hit","Sample-ratio mismatch; rebalance users between the experiment variants"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Thumbs-down cases are useful failure evidence but do not represent the full production distribution. A stratified sample across task types and feedback states preserves failure coverage without making the eval set predict only dissatisfied-user traffic." },
{ id:"d4-30", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Leadership demands 'zero incorrect answers' from a generative system. How should the architect reframe this using SLO thinking?",
  options:["Keep a 100% quality SLO and treat every miss as an outage","Define a measurable quality floor, risk-weighted error budget, and escalation path","Use average satisfaction as the sole SLO and review outliers quarterly","Replace the quality target with an availability SLO for the serving API"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A production SLO should define the measurable quality floor, the tolerated residual risk, and what happens when confidence or impact requires abstention or human review. Availability and satisfaction can be guardrails, but neither substitutes for a quality objective." },
{ id:"d4-31", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Two safety thresholds have similar aggregate results, but traffic includes low-risk FAQs and high-impact account actions. What analysis is needed before choosing an operating point?",
  options:["Compare only the overall harmful-pass rate because impact is already reflected in traffic volume","Compare only overrefusal because legitimate users provide the most observable feedback","Report both error types with uncertainty by risk tier and test them against predeclared limits","Select the threshold with the lowest unweighted average across the two aggregate error rates"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Similar aggregate rates can conceal very different consequences across task tiers. The evaluation must report harmful passes and overrefusals, their uncertainty, and their risk-tier distribution against criteria declared before results are inspected; one-sided or unweighted aggregates can hide unacceptable behavior." },
{ id:"d4-32", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A tool-using RAG assistant must be evaluated for prompt-injection resistance. Which test plan provides the strongest evidence?",
  options:["Run direct user-prompt attacks and report the assistant's refusal rate","Test direct and indirect attacks across retrieval and tool paths, measuring attack success and legitimate-task false positives","Use provider harmlessness benchmarks and the assistant's ordinary task-accuracy suite","Apply a toxicity classifier to outputs and manually review a sample of blocked requests"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A security eval must exercise direct and indirect injection through the paths that can carry untrusted instructions or trigger actions. Measuring both attack success and false positives on legitimate tasks captures security and utility; refusal-only, generic benchmark, or toxicity-only plans omit critical paths or outcomes." },
{ id:"d4-33", domain:4, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"The same fixed regression queries now produce more wrong raw outputs. Scoring, timeout policy, application code, prompt, and pinned Claude model ID are unchanged. Which TWO external causes should be investigated?",
  options:["The upstream retrieval corpus or connector changed content or structure","Production traffic shifted toward longer user requests","The reporting dashboard changed its aggregation window","The test scheduler now runs at a different time of day","Serving-layer behavior or another external dependency changed"],
  correct:[0,4],
  explanation:"A fixed regression set removes live traffic mix from the diagnosis, but its retrieved evidence can still change when an upstream corpus or connector changes. Claude 4.6+ and Claude 5 model IDs are pinned snapshots; model weights stay fixed, while serving infrastructure or another dependency can still alter observable behavior." },
{ id:"d4-34", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A RAG assistant must measure retrieval coverage, answer faithfulness, and compliance with crisp policy rules. Which mixed evaluation design is strongest?",
  options:["Use exact-match checks for retrieval, faithfulness, and every policy decision","Use one LLM grader for every criterion, calibrated once before launch","Use code for retrieval and policy, a calibrated faithfulness grader, and sampled human audits","Use sampled human review for every criterion and remove automated scoring"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Deterministic retrieval and policy properties should use reliable code checks. Faithfulness requires a nuanced rubric-based grader whose reliability is established and periodically audited with sampled human judgments; neither one universal method nor full manual review is appropriate." },
{ id:"d4-35", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"An experiment planned 50/50 random assignment, but eligible traffic appears 67/33 with no documented allocation change. What should the team do before interpreting quality results?",
  options:["Reweight the observed results and ship the leading variant","Extend the experiment until the raw counts happen to become equal","Investigate randomization and logging as a sample-ratio mismatch","Report only the larger arm because it has the narrower interval"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"A large unexplained allocation imbalance can signal broken randomization, eligibility handling, or instrumentation. The team should diagnose that sample-ratio mismatch before trusting treatment estimates; reweighting or waiting does not repair an unknown assignment defect." },
{ id:"d4-36", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A planned non-inferiority test meets its prespecified quality margin at the required sample size. Safety and p95 latency remain within guardrails, and cost per request falls 40%. What should the team do?",
  options:["Reject it because production changes require significant quality superiority over the incumbent","Adopt through a monitored rollout because quality and guardrails held while cost fell","Repeat until quality superiority is significant even though non-inferiority was planned","Keep both configurations in permanent rotation to avoid a cost-quality decision"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"The experiment established the decision it was designed to test: any quality loss is within the acceptable margin, guardrails remain satisfied, and cost is materially lower. A monitored rollout captures production dynamics without demanding an unrelated superiority result." },
{ id:"d4-37", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"Why should a team define evals before writing production code, rather than waiting until after launch?",
  options:["To define acceptance criteria before implementation choices can bias the target","To lock the technical architecture before stakeholders define outcomes","To maximize a public benchmark score before collecting user requirements","To avoid revising the evaluation set after production traffic becomes available, keeping results comparable across releases"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Predefined success criteria make completion and regression measurable before the team optimizes toward observed outputs. Architecture still follows requirements, public benchmarks do not replace task-specific evidence, and production traffic should continue to improve the evaluation set." },
{ id:"d4-38", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"End-to-end p95 latency is 4 s against a 3 s budget. What is the first diagnostic step?",
  options:["Compare model latency with prior releases before inspecting other stages","Break latency down stage by stage before optimizing","Tune retrieval latency first because it adds a separate service call","Increase the timeout and monitor abandonment before changing components"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Latency work starts with end-to-end attribution so the team optimizes the stage that owns the overrun. Starting with the model or retrieval assumes a culprit, while increasing a timeout changes failure handling without identifying the source of the p95 breach." },
{ id:"d4-39", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A workload writes cache entries for a stable prefix, but most entries expire before reuse. Usage shows many cache-creation tokens, few cache-read tokens, and higher total input cost. What should the team conclude?",
  options:["Keep caching because cache creation confirms the prefix was accepted","Reuse is too low to offset cache writes; change eligibility or TTL and remeasure","Add more breakpoints so each request creates additional cached prefixes","Move dynamic request content before the stable prefix to improve isolation"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A cacheable prefix does not guarantee savings. Cache writes carry a premium and reads provide the discount, so a creation-heavy, read-light path can cost more; the team should change which requests are cached or the applicable TTL, then verify read rate, latency, and total cost." },
{ id:"d4-40", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A fix for an unusual production input has been deployed. Which evidence is sufficient to close the incident?",
  options:["Unit, integration, and replay tests pass in the test environment","The regression passes, canary metrics recover, and the owner approves closure","The incident is archived after one quiet production hour and no new alerts, confirming the fix held under real traffic","No additional users report the issue before the next operations review"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Closure requires evidence at both evaluation and production levels: the original failure is encoded and passes, a limited rollout shows the affected live metrics recovering, and an accountable owner records the decision. Unit tests or silence alone do not establish end-to-end recovery." }
]);

// Hard set
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id:"d4-41", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An automated code reviewer flags dozens of issues per PR, but half are false positives: patterns the team deliberately uses, internal frameworks it misreads, and style the project accepts. Developers have started ignoring it. What is the fix?",
  options:["Raise the severity threshold so only the most confident findings surface, restoring developer attention to the reviews","Supply the project's conventions, accepted patterns, and exclusion criteria as persistent review context","Route all findings through a human filter before developers see them","Replace the reviewer with a stricter linter for objective rules only"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"The reviewer is judging against generic norms because nobody gave it the project's; persistent project context removes the false-positive class at the source. Thresholding (A) drops true findings along with false ones, human pre-filtering (C) pays people to compensate for missing context, and a linter (D) abandons the semantic reviews that motivated the tool." },
{ id:"d4-42", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An extraction system performs well on invoice fields but unreliably on handwritten margin notes — yet it keeps producing margin-note extractions that downstream teams mistake for dependable data. What is the prompt-level control?",
  options:["Lower the temperature for the unreliable category to reduce its variance","Add more margin-note examples until reliability improves across the handwriting variants seen in production","Attach a global disclaimer that some extractions may be imperfect","Define explicit inclusion and exclusion boundaries so the unreliable category is not extracted at all"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Where performance is known-unreliable, the honest control is exclusion: scope the task so the system does not generate findings it cannot stand behind. Temperature (A) narrows variance around unreliable output, more examples (B) is a bet while bad data keeps flowing, and disclaimers (C) label the problem without stopping it." },
{ id:"d4-43", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Generated tests for a legacy module all pass, but review shows most assert trivialities — functions return without throwing, values equal themselves. What input makes the next generation round produce meaningful tests?",
  options:["Existing test files, the team's fixture conventions, and explicit criteria separating behavioral tests from trivial assertions","A doubled test-count target so real tests appear among the volume","The module's full commit history so tests reflect its evolution","A coverage percentage threshold the generated suite must reach before merging, since coverage is the metric stakeholders track"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Test quality is specified, not hoped for: exemplar tests, fixture conventions, and a stated triviality bar define what 'good' means for this codebase. Volume (B) multiplies trivial assertions, commit history (C) adds context without criteria, and coverage thresholds (D) are satisfiable by exactly the trivial tests under complaint." },
{ id:"d4-44", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A document-extraction service has review capacity for 6% of volume. Errors on payment fields are costly; most other fields are tolerant. Extractions carry confidence scores. How should review be routed?",
  options:["Randomly sample 6% of documents so the review set stays statistically unbiased","Review the first documents of each day until capacity is exhausted","By expected harm: low-confidence extractions and sensitive fields first, plus a small random slice for drift","Review every document from customers above a revenue threshold"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Routing by confidence and field-level stakes puts scarce review where errors cost most, while a residual random slice preserves drift detection — random-only (A) is the strongest distractor because it sounds statistically virtuous while spending most capacity on harmless fields. FIFO (B) and revenue-based (D) correlate with nothing that predicts error or harm." },
{ id:"d4-45", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A judge model scores the eval suite at 92% — but several golden-set cases also appear verbatim as few-shot examples in the production prompt being evaluated. What is the methodological problem?",
  options:["The judge should score more granularly than pass/fail","Contamination: eval cases appearing as prompt exemplars inflate the scores — the two sets must be disjoint","The golden set is too small for a stable 92% estimate","The judge and the generator should come from different model families to avoid self-preference bias in the scores"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Cases the prompt already contains measure recall of the exemplar, not generalization: the score is structurally inflated regardless of judge quality. Granularity (A), sample size (C), and judge independence (D) are real methodology concerns that leave this specific defect — train/test overlap by another name — untouched." },
{ id:"d4-46", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An A/B test on a new retrieval configuration shows the primary quality metric up 4 points with adequate sample size — and the latency guardrail up 35%, breaching the SLA at p95. What is the correct decision?",
  options:["Ship it: the primary metric was the pre-registered decision criterion, and adding conditions afterwards moves the goalposts","Extend the test until latency regresses to the mean","Ship to executive-visible accounts first, where quality matters most","Do not ship as-is: a guardrail breach vetoes the primary win — iterate on the configuration's cost before re-testing"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Guardrails exist precisely to veto wins that break something else; a breached SLA is a failed launch condition, not a footnote. A misreads pre-registration as covering only the primary; B hopes a real cost regresses like noise; C ships the breach to the most visible users first." },
{ id:"d4-47", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A generation workflow produces flawed drafts, and the team's feedback loop — replying 'improve this' and regenerating — is not converging. What does an effective iterative refinement loop look like?",
  options:["Concrete input-output examples of target quality, feedback naming each failure, and batched issues per iteration","Full regeneration from scratch each round, so no error from any prior draft can ever accumulate into the next one","The same request at increasing reasoning effort until quality appears","A second model rewriting the first model's drafts each round"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Refinement converges when the signal is specific: exemplars define the target, named failures direct the change, and batching consolidates evaluation. Scratch regeneration (B) discards what each round got right, effort escalation (C) spends more thinking on the same under-specified ask, and a rewriter (D) adds a second opinion with the same missing signal." },
{ id:"d4-48", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"After an index refresh, global accuracy dips only 1.5 points — within tolerance — but complaints concentrate sharply. Segmented evals show contract-type documents dropped 22 points while everything else held. What does this demonstrate about metric design?",
  options:["Global averages need wider tolerance bands after infrastructure changes","Complaint volume is a leading indicator that should replace lagging accuracy metrics in the operational dashboard","Aggregate metrics mask localized regressions: reporting must segment by slice with per-segment thresholds","The 22-point segment is too small to matter if the average holds"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"A catastrophic segment failure hid inside an acceptable average — the standing argument for sliced metrics with per-segment gates. Wider tolerance (A) hides more, complaints-as-metric (B) makes users the monitoring, and D dismisses the exact population the incident is harming." }
]);

// Hard set v2
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id:"d4-49", domain:4, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A CI gate currently blocks deploys when global eval accuracy drops. After the contract-segment incident, which TWO gate changes are correct?",
  options:["Lower the global threshold so fewer deploys block","Raise the global threshold to catch regressions earlier","Per-slice acceptance thresholds, so a localized collapse blocks even when the aggregate holds","Replace the gate with a post-deploy review meeting","Slice definitions maintained with the eval set, so new document types get gates as they enter production"],
  correct:[2,4],
  explanation:"Segment failures need segment gates, and gates only cover what slice definitions keep current. Moving the global threshold in either direction (A, B) still averages away localized collapse, and meetings after deploy (D) review what a gate should have stopped." },
{ id:"d4-50", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team runs three judge models per eval item and majority-votes, tripling eval cost. Judge-human agreement was never measured. What is the disciplined alternative?",
  options:["Calibrate one judge against human labels first; add ensemble members only where disagreement persists","Five judges: larger ensembles are strictly more reliable","Keep three judges but rotate them weekly for freshness","Drop to the cheapest single judge to fund more eval items, trading judge quality for evaluation breadth"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Ensembling treats variance, and nobody measured whether variance is the problem: calibration against ground truth comes first, with ensemble spend targeted at proven disagreement. Bigger ensembles (B) scale an unvalidated remedy, rotation (C) adds churn, and cheapest-first (D) optimizes cost before validity." },
{ id:"d4-51", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Two prompt variants produce answers whose absolute 1–10 quality scores overlap heavily, and human raters use the scale inconsistently. The decision is simply which variant is better. Which evaluation design fits?",
  options:["Anchor the 1–10 scale with a detailed rubric and re-rate","Have the two variant authors debate the differences","Average the noisy scores across enough items to stabilize, letting the law of large numbers absorb the rater inconsistency","Pairwise comparison: raters see both outputs per input and pick the better one, aggregated into a preference rate"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Which-is-better questions are answered by pairwise preference, where humans are far more consistent than on absolute scales. Rubric anchoring (A) improves the harder instrument instead of switching to the right one, averaging noise (C) stabilizes around scale inconsistency, and debate (B) measures persuasion." },
{ id:"d4-52", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A PM checks a running A/B test daily and wants to stop the moment significance appears. Statistically, what is wrong, and what is the sound alternative?",
  options:["Nothing: statistical significance is significance whenever it appears, and waiting only delays a decision already made","Daily peeking inflates false positives; fix the horizon in advance or use a sequential design built for interim looks","Peeking is fine if only the PM sees the dashboard","The test should run exactly one week regardless of size"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Optional stopping is the classic way tests lie: each look is another draw at a threshold calibrated for one. The remedies are pre-fixed horizons or sequential methods designed for interim analysis. A blesses the inflation, C confuses access with statistics, and D substitutes ritual for power analysis." },
{ id:"d4-53", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Baseline task success is 80%, and the smallest improvement worth shipping is 3 points. An engineer proposes 150 conversations per arm. What is the correct reading?",
  options:["Adequate: 150 per arm is a common industry rule of thumb","Overkill: differences this small are visible by inspection of a few dozen transcripts by an experienced human reviewer","Only long conversations need large samples","Underpowered by a wide margin: detecting a 3-point shift near 80% reliably needs on the order of thousands per arm"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Small effects need large samples: at these rates, 150 per arm has little chance of distinguishing a real 3-point gain from noise. Rules of thumb (A) and inspection (B) are how underpowered wins get shipped, and conversation length (C) is not the variable that sets n." },
{ id:"d4-54", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A canary deployment needs automatic rollback. Which trigger-metric set is correct?",
  options:["Fast, low-noise proxies with tight thresholds — error rate, refusal spikes, schema violations, latency — for automatic rollback","Overall user satisfaction, the metric leadership tracks","Token consumption per request, the most sensitive signal available, since any behavioral change moves it before anything else does","Any single anomaly on any dashboard triggers rollback"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Auto-rollback triggers must move fast and false-alarm rarely: operational proxies qualify, while satisfaction (B) lags days and cannot gate minutes. Token counts (C) are sensitive to everything including improvements, and any-anomaly triggers (D) make the canary flap on noise." },
{ id:"d4-55", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Offline evals improved 6 points across three releases, but production task completion has not moved. What is the correct interpretation and response?",
  options:["Production users are misusing the system; retrain them","The proxy has decoupled from the outcome: audit eval-traffic match and metric validity, then re-anchor the suite","Six points is within noise; keep shipping","Offline evals are worthless; move to production-only measurement, where production reality is the only honest judge"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A proxy that rises while the outcome stands still has lost validity — through drift in traffic, saturation, or optimization against the metric. Blaming users (A) and dismissing the gap (C) ignore the decoupling, and abandoning offline evals (D) trades a repairable instrument for flying blind between releases." },
{ id:"d4-56", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Two configurations: A costs $0.021 per request with 71% task success; B costs $0.034 with 89%. Failed tasks route to humans at ~$1.40. Which comparison metric decides?",
  options:["Cost per request: A wins decisively, running at only 62% of B's unit price per call","Task success: B wins by 18 points","Cost per successful task including failure handling: A ≈ $0.44, B ≈ $0.19","Run both and let finance choose a lens"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Unit economics must include the cost of failure: folding the $1.40 fallback in reverses the per-request ranking decisively. A prices requests rather than outcomes, B counts quality without money, and D outsources the metric decision the analysis exists to make." },
{ id:"d4-57", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Users report the assistant 'got slower'. TTFT is unchanged at 480 ms, but total response time rose 40%. What does this signature indicate?",
  options:["Network degradation between client and platform","Retrieval latency growth ahead of the model call as the corpus expands","Cache hit rate collapse on the prompt prefix","The slowdown lives in generation length or throughput"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Stable TTFT clears everything before first token: network (A), retrieval (B), and cache (C) all show up in TTFT. What grew is the part after — output volume or streaming throughput — which is where the diagnosis goes." },
{ id:"d4-58", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A load test fires 500 identical short requests per minute and declares the system ready for launch. Why is this test invalid for an LLM workload, and what is the correct design?",
  options:["The rate is too low; double it and re-run","Identical requests are fine; determinism aids comparison","Load tests are unnecessary once the provider publishes rate limits, which already define the system's real ceiling","LLM capacity is token-shaped and cache-sensitive: identical short requests hit warm caches and minimal tokens"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Requests-per-minute is the wrong unit when cost and latency scale with tokens and cache state: the synthetic test measured a workload production will never send. Doubling the wrong load (A) and celebrating its determinism (B) refine the irrelevance, and published limits (C) bound the platform, not your pipeline." },
{ id:"d4-59", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"The team wants early warning when incoming request content shifts away from what the eval set represents. Which monitor design is correct?",
  options:["Alert when request volume deviates from forecast, the earliest and cheapest available signal that something changed","Embed incoming requests and compare their distribution against the eval set's, alerting on sustained divergence","Alert when average request length changes by 10%","Re-run the eval suite hourly to catch the shift"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Content drift is measured in content space: embedding-distribution divergence against the eval baseline detects shifts volume (A) and length (C) proxies miss. Re-running a static suite faster (D) measures the old distribution more often — the drift is in what the suite no longer contains." },
{ id:"d4-60", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Two annotators label the eval ground truth. On a shared calibration sample their agreement is barely above chance. What must happen before their labels gate anything?",
  options:["Average their labels: disagreement cancels out","Keep the annotator whose labels favor the current system","Fix the instrument: tighten the guideline, adjudicate the disagreement cases","Add a third annotator as a tiebreaker over the same guideline, resolving disagreements by majority"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Near-chance agreement means the labeling task is under-specified: the guideline, not the headcount, is broken. Averaging (A) launders noise into authority, picking the favorable annotator (B) is selection bias as process, and a third rater on the same broken guideline (D) tiebreaks randomness." },
{ id:"d4-61", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A model upgrade legitimately changes preferred phrasings, and dozens of golden-set reference answers now read outdated even where the new outputs are fine. What is the correct maintenance response?",
  options:["Version the golden set: review flagged references, update those where the new behavior is genuinely acceptable with recorded rationale","Regenerate all references with the new model for consistency","Freeze the references: a golden set that changes with every model release is no standard, only a moving target that measures nothing stable","Delete style-sensitive cases, keeping only exact-match items"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Golden sets are versioned instruments: deliberate, recorded updates keep them fair without dissolving the standard. Regenerating from the system under test (B) makes the exam grade itself, freezing (C) turns style drift into permanent false failures, and deleting nuance (D) shrinks the suite to what was never at issue." },
{ id:"d4-62", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Red-teaming happens before each major launch, then stops. Between launches, prompt and retrieval changes ship weekly. What is the structural gap?",
  options:["Red-teaming should also cover the office network","Distill red-team findings into an adversarial regression suite running in CI","Red-teamers should be rotated more frequently","Weekly changes are too small to affect security posture in any measurable way"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Point-in-time exercises secure points in time: continuous change needs the findings converted into automated adversarial regression, with humans refreshing the suite's imagination. Network scope (A) and rotation (C) tune the exercise without closing the cadence gap, and D is the assumption injections exploit." },
{ id:"d4-63", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A faithfulness eval must catch summaries that assert things their source documents do not support. Which construction is correct?",
  options:["Compare each summary against a reference summary written by an expert on the same source","Score summaries on fluency and coherence with a calibrated judge","Ask the generating model whether its summary is faithful","Decompose each summary into atomic claims and verify each against the source"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Faithfulness is claim-level entailment against the source: decomposition catches the single invented fact a holistic score forgives. Reference comparison (A) measures similarity to one telling, fluency (B) rewards confident fabrication, and self-assessment (C) asks the defendant." },
{ id:"d4-64", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Quality regressed after a release that changed the prompt, the retrieval top-k, and the model version together. What does disciplined regression triage look like?",
  options:["Bisect the configuration space: re-run evals toggling one dimension at a time from the last good configuration","Revert all three changes and re-ship them one per week, isolating each change's effect on real production traffic","Ask the engineers which change feels most likely","Accept the regression until the next scheduled release"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Attribution comes from controlled variation against the last good state — cheap to run with an eval suite. Full revert with calendar-paced re-landing (B) is the slow version of the same bisection, intuition polling (C) is how the wrong change gets fixed, and D leaves users holding the regression." },
{ id:"d4-65", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Two teams unknowingly ran overlapping experiments on the same traffic: a routing test and a prompt test. Both declared wins. What is the methodological problem?",
  options:["Nothing: independent teams randomize independently, so each test's assignment stays statistically unbiased","Interference: each test's arms contain a shifting mix of the other's variants, biasing both estimates","Both wins should be halved as a correction","The larger team's result takes precedence"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Uncoordinated overlap contaminates both estimates unless independence is engineered — via layered exclusive slices or explicit factorial analysis. Independent randomization (A) does not remove interaction effects, halving (C) is a made-up correction, and seniority (D) is not statistics." },
{ id:"d4-66", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An engineer adds per-user, per-prompt-version, per-document metric labels, and the observability bill and dashboard latency explode. What is the correct telemetry design?",
  options:["Remove all labels and keep global aggregates only","Bound cardinality by design: low-cardinality dimensions (version, route, model, segment) on metrics","Buy a bigger observability tier and keep every label, since detail lost now cannot be recovered later","Rotate which labels are active each week"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Metrics carry dimensions you aggregate by; identities live in traces and logs. Global-only (A) reproduces the averages-hide-slices failure, paying for unbounded cardinality (C) subsidizes the design error, and rotating labels (D) makes every week's dashboards incomparable." },
{ id:"d4-67", domain:4, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Incident retros keep filing failures under 'model was wrong', and fixes don't accumulate. What does a useful error taxonomy look like?",
  options:["Fewer categories, so filing is fast and consistent","Categories by architectural cause, each mapped to an owning component and a standard first diagnostic","Categories by user segment, since impact differs by audience and remediation priorities should follow impact","Free-text descriptions, since categories constrain nuance"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"A taxonomy earns its keep when categories map to causes, owners, and next diagnostic steps — turning incident counts into a fix backlog. Fewer buckets (A) and free text (D) fail in opposite directions, and segmenting by audience (C) organizes impact while leaving causes unnamed." }
]);
